  "Edward Westray, a young architect, is sent to the remote town of Cullerne to supervise the restoration of Cullerne Minster. Drawn into the life of the town, he hears rumours of a mystery surrounding the claim to the title of Lord Blandamer, whose coat of arms, the nebuly coat of the title, fills the great transept window of the Minster. When the new Lord Blandamer arrives and promises to pay all the costs of the restoration, Westray comes to suspect that he is not what he seems.",
  "Falkner's own interests in church architecture and heraldry: the Romanesque arches of Cullerne Minster recall those of Durham Cathedral, which he knew as Honorary Librarian to the Dean and Chapter and saw from his house on Palace Green, and Jacques Heyman has suggested that the plot was inspired by the collapse of the central tower of Chichester Cathedral in 1861.",
  "Reviewed in the Spectator and the Leeds Mercury in November 1903, reprinted by Penguin in 1943 and by Oxford University Press in 1954 and 1988, and adapted for BBC radio in 1965, The Nebuly Coat has been reissued repeatedly; A. N. Wilson, in the Telegraph in 2004, warned that it 'could strike the careless reader as no more than a curiosity, a bit of amateur work. Yet this would be a mistake.'",
